WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.960 --> 00:00:11.400
Since Uber invade our business it messed up
my life.

00:00:11.400 --> 00:00:14.900
It is not a real bailout because we're not
asking for a penny.

00:00:14.900 --> 00:00:18.360
We want big papa paying attention to us.

00:00:29.580 --> 00:00:35.480
When Andrew Murstein hired pop music
star Nicki Minaj to headline his son Matt’s Bar Mitzvah,

00:00:35.480 --> 00:00:38.820
it was an unusually conspicuous flaunting
of wealth

00:00:38.820 --> 00:00:42.160
by one of New York City's leading taxi cab magnates.

00:00:42.160 --> 00:00:47.120
Newsweek estimates that Minaj might have been
paid as much as half a million for the gig,

00:00:47.120 --> 00:00:52.860
which entailed taking a personal photo with
every single prepubescent boy in attendance.

00:00:52.860 --> 00:00:56.560
Andrew Murstein’s vast wealth derives from
a shrewd decision

00:00:56.560 --> 00:01:00.860
that his grandpa Leon made 78 years ago.

00:01:00.860 --> 00:01:09.160
In 1937, New York City issued what ultimately
amounted to 13,437 taxi medallions—

00:01:09.160 --> 00:01:14.060
a special kind of license that conferred the
exclusive right to drive a cab in the Big Apple.

00:01:14.060 --> 00:01:17.400
And Leon Murstein bought one for $10.

00:01:17.400 --> 00:01:21.040
And then he bought several hundred more to
rent to other drivers.

00:01:21.040 --> 00:01:27.560
Over the next 75 years price of each of those
medallions climbed from $10 to over a million dollars.

00:01:27.560 --> 00:01:32.420
Ultimately all that value that’s built up
in the medallions comes out of passengers.

00:01:32.420 --> 00:01:36.620
Josh Barro is a reporter at the The New York
Times, who covers the taxi industry.

00:01:36.620 --> 00:01:39.460
Fares are higher than they otherwise would
be able to be

00:01:39.460 --> 00:01:43.520
because you have to limit the number of people
using these taxis.

00:01:46.140 --> 00:01:50.460
Over the years, the city tried periodically
to break the medallion cartel

00:01:50.460 --> 00:01:54.080
to make life easier on the riding public,
but to no avail.

00:01:54.080 --> 00:01:56.300
And then in May of 2011,

00:01:56.300 --> 00:01:58.240
A new company rolled into town.

00:01:58.240 --> 00:02:02.660
Welcome to Uber, a reliable ride is always
just minutes away.

00:02:02.660 --> 00:02:05.120
Let's get one.

00:02:05.120 --> 00:02:08.560
These are all the cars in your area and how
close they are.

00:02:08.560 --> 00:02:13.340
For roughly two and a half years after Uber's
arrival, the taxi industry kept thriving.

00:02:13.340 --> 00:02:16.720
In November 2013, Medallion Financial,

00:02:16.720 --> 00:02:20.060
the publicly traded company that’s run by
Andrew Murstein

00:02:20.060 --> 00:02:22.620
saw its share price hit a thirteen-year high.

00:02:22.620 --> 00:02:26.520
But as more and more Uber cars took to the
streets the stock plummeted,

00:02:26.520 --> 00:02:29.140
ultimately losing about half of its value.

00:02:29.140 --> 00:02:32.240
The average number of trips taken in a cab
each day has fallen

00:02:32.240 --> 00:02:34.580
and average farebox revenue has fallen.

00:02:34.590 --> 00:02:38.460
The front of this taxi garage in Brooklyn
is jammed with parked cabs

00:02:38.460 --> 00:02:41.740
because the company that runs it can’t find
enough drivers to hire.

00:02:41.740 --> 00:02:43.540
They’d all rather go work for Uber.

00:02:43.540 --> 00:02:47.660
It really is remarkable how these monopolies
that couldn’t be broken for decades

00:02:47.660 --> 00:02:48.840
were broken so quickly

00:02:48.840 --> 00:02:51.340
but I think it’s because of Uber moving
first

00:02:51.340 --> 00:02:53.880
and saying, ‘we’re going show people how
this thing can be different,

00:02:53.880 --> 00:02:57.900
and then we’ll have the fight over whether
we want that different thing or not.'

00:02:57.900 --> 00:03:01.060
The Mursteins diversified out of the medallion
market long ago,

00:03:01.060 --> 00:03:03.420
so they’ll end up rich no matter what happens.

00:03:03.420 --> 00:03:07.140
But Gene Friedman, the city’s largest single
medallion holder

00:03:07.140 --> 00:03:10.000
with an estimated 900 to his name,

00:03:10.000 --> 00:03:11.440
is in deep trouble.

00:03:11.440 --> 00:03:16.640
He has been sued by the attorney general for
ripping off drivers, flagged as a tax delinquent,

00:03:16.640 --> 00:03:20.820
and a handful of the taxi garages he owns
have filed for bankruptcy protection.

00:03:20.820 --> 00:03:23.880
In April, Friedman arranged a private meeting
with bankers,

00:03:23.880 --> 00:03:26.000
medallion owners, and elected officials

00:03:26.000 --> 00:03:28.020
to build support for a city bailout.

00:03:28.020 --> 00:03:31.900
It’s a scream by a child for attention.

00:03:31.900 --> 00:03:33.000
What you're doing is a scream?

00:03:33.000 --> 00:03:33.860
That's correct?

00:03:33.860 --> 00:03:34.640
From a child?

00:03:34.640 --> 00:03:35.360
That's right.

00:03:35.360 --> 00:03:37.000
This entire industry is a child?

00:03:37.000 --> 00:03:38.020
No, you, that's not the-

00:03:38.020 --> 00:03:40.220
We are absolute- we're all children of the
city.

00:03:40.220 --> 00:03:44.260
We're all children of the city and the city
council, and of city hall.

00:03:44.260 --> 00:03:48.120
His idea is that the government should step
in and offer a loan guarantee

00:03:48.120 --> 00:03:52.180
so taxpayers would take the hit if medallion
prices were to keep tumbling.

00:03:52.180 --> 00:03:57.300
It is not a real bailout because we’re not
asking for a penny here.

00:03:57.300 --> 00:04:01.380
The problem is that if the city is guaranteeing
a repayment on a medallion loan

00:04:01.380 --> 00:04:04.320
of a level exceeding the actual market price
of a taxi medallion,

00:04:04.320 --> 00:04:08.180
and the city is going to be left on the hook
for millions and millions

00:04:08.180 --> 00:04:12.180
and conceivably billions of dollars in lost
value on medallions.

00:04:12.180 --> 00:04:15.820
Now what people like Gene would say is that
there's just a temporary disruption in this market

00:04:15.820 --> 00:04:17.740
it's like 2008 after the financial crisis

00:04:17.740 --> 00:04:21.820
and if the government just steps in and acts
as a guarantor, things will go back to normal.

00:04:21.820 --> 00:04:23.780
That's not what's happening in the taxi space.

00:04:23.780 --> 00:04:25.420
There's an actual solvency problem,

00:04:25.420 --> 00:04:28.620
which is that the real value of a taxi medallion
has declined

00:04:28.620 --> 00:04:33.840
because you can not generate as much profit
off of a taxi medallion as you used to be able to.

00:04:33.840 --> 00:04:37.750
I used to work hard to get my dream to come
true in this country.

00:04:37.750 --> 00:04:40.340
Sweet land of liberty, yes.

00:04:40.340 --> 00:04:42.300
Alaa Khalil is an Egyptian immigrant

00:04:42.300 --> 00:04:46.300
who spent years driving someone else's cab
about 90 hours a week.

00:04:46.300 --> 00:04:51.480
In 1994, he’d finally saved enough to make
a down payment on his very own medallion,

00:04:51.480 --> 00:04:54.460
which cost $178,000.

00:04:54.460 --> 00:05:00.300
Between 94 and 2013, it was a very good life.

00:05:00.300 --> 00:05:07.160
Since Uber invade our business, it messed
up my life.

00:05:07.160 --> 00:05:10.720
Less fares to the airports, they took all
the airports.

00:05:10.720 --> 00:05:14.220
And we used to see a lot of people in the
street

00:05:14.220 --> 00:05:18.480
and now people are waiting in front of their
houses with the iphones

00:05:18.480 --> 00:05:25.380
looking for the Uber to come, and they never
hail us anymore.

00:05:25.380 --> 00:05:29.860
I'm in financial crush, I stopped payment
on all of my credit cards.

00:05:29.860 --> 00:05:34.380
What if we lose this house, what about my
kids, where are they gonna live?

00:05:34.380 --> 00:05:35.760
It's really hard.

00:05:35.760 --> 00:05:40.640
Selling his medallion is no longer an option
because there’s no liquidity in the market.

00:05:40.640 --> 00:05:44.020
Nobody even knows what a medallion would sell
for at this point

00:05:44.020 --> 00:05:47.420
because in the past four months there hasn’t
been a single sale.

00:05:47.420 --> 00:05:49.680
Just five bank foreclosures.

00:05:49.680 --> 00:05:54.820
They're going to strip my medallion if I don't
pay like 2 months

00:05:54.820 --> 00:05:57.780
they take your car, while you're sleeping
inside.

00:05:57.780 --> 00:06:02.380
The people I would worry about are these individual
cab operators who own one medallion

00:06:02.380 --> 00:06:05.220
who tied up their life savings in the medallion,
it's sad

00:06:05.220 --> 00:06:08.060
and I think that there are, you know, there
are real people who got hurt here.

00:06:08.060 --> 00:06:12.300
But again I would remember that the value
in those medallions was not free.

00:06:12.300 --> 00:06:17.680
The value in those medallions came out of
elevated prices payed by consumers of taxi rides.

00:06:17.680 --> 00:06:19.740
Alaa Khalil wouldn’t be in so much trouble

00:06:19.740 --> 00:06:23.420
if he hadn’t treated his medallion like
a cash register over the years.

00:06:23.420 --> 00:06:28.120
As its price soared, he took vacations, bought
an expensive house, and a luxury car.

00:06:28.120 --> 00:06:32.900
Overall, he borrowed an additional $300,000
against its value.

00:06:32.900 --> 00:06:35.160
I refinanced in the medallion too many times.

00:06:35.160 --> 00:06:37.420
I mean, like three, four times.

00:06:37.420 --> 00:06:43.340
Every time I need money, I needed money, I
go and pick up like 50 thousand, 40 thousand,

00:06:43.340 --> 00:06:47.180
it was a lot of money in the medallion, now
I have nothing inside.

00:06:49.780 --> 00:06:52.300
This is an argument for why you shouldn't
create a medallion system.

00:06:52.300 --> 00:06:58.200
Not every city exposes small investors to
this business of making big bets in public policy

00:06:58.200 --> 00:07:02.860
and we should try and build our economy not
around the government creating extra opportunities

00:07:02.860 --> 00:07:05.640
for people to make those sorts of unwise investments.

00:07:05.640 --> 00:07:09.139
Uber's been unequivocally good for taxi customers

00:07:09.140 --> 00:07:13.240
and as even Khalil acknowledges, it's also
been good for many drivers.

00:07:13.240 --> 00:07:16.180
It's a good opportunity for many immigrants,
yes, yes.

00:07:16.180 --> 00:07:22.039
Because they don't, they're going to find
a new job and fast, and make money fast.

00:07:22.040 --> 00:07:29.040
My nephew is an Uber, he's been here like
5 years now and he makes as much as I make now.

00:07:29.040 --> 00:07:34.360
I'm happy for him, but I'm not happy for my
family.

